<!-- SLIDE 2 -->
<div class="dtexp-board-slide">
 <div class="dtexp-head"><h2>Our upsell isn't strategic yet — it rides the customer's income</h2><span class="dtexp-per">Discussion topic · 2/3 · Upsell</span></div>
 <div class="dtexp-body">
  <div class="dtexp-eye">What moves net upsell · plan changes vs. customer income (FX-neutral)</div>
  <div class="dtexp-two">
    <div id="dtexp-chart2"></div>
    <div class="dtexp-stat">
      <div class="dtexp-big">~6 in 10<span> of upsell reverses</span></div>
      <p style="margin:9px 0 0">The scatter shows net upsell <b>rising in high-income months and falling in low-income ones</b> — much of it is customers <b>crossing a billing threshold</b> in a strong month, not us delivering more value. It's seasonal (peaks in <b>December</b>, drops in <b>January</b>).</p>
      <p>So the same accounts bounce up and back down: <b>most upsell comes from accounts that also downgrade later</b> (chronic oscillators). It's oscillation on the customer's income cycle — not durable expansion.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="dtexp-take"><b>Today's upsell isn't something we drive — it's the customer's income cycle.</b> They upgrade when they cross a billing limit in a good month, then revert when income drops. The strategic shift: move from <b>upsell by billing</b> to <b>upsell by value</b> — tie upgrades to features and usage, not the customer's revenue.</div>
 </div>
 <div class="dtexp-foot"><span>Alegra · Confidential · fact_customers_mrr (plan moves) × income_by_logo (FX-neutralized)</span><span>Expansion · 2/3</span></div>
</div>

<!-- SLIDE 3 -->
<div class="dtexp-board-slide">
 <div class="dtexp-head"><h2>We need to build the expansion machine</h2><span class="dtexp-per">Discussion topic · 3/3 · The strategy</span></div>
 <div class="dtexp-body">
  <div class="dtexp-eye">The strategy · pricing + product + process, working together</div>
  <p class="dtexp-lead">No single lever fixes this. Expansion has to become a <b>system</b> — pricing, product and the commercial team giving and using clear value levers, not one-off reactions.</p>
  <div class="dtexp-cards">
    <div class="dtexp-card"><div class="dtexp-t"><span class="dtexp-num">1</span>Be intentional with pricing</div><div class="dtexp-d">Our strongest lever. Easing this year was a <b>deliberate call</b> — and the data says the churn we feared from price increases does not necessarily stick. So there's room to push. The question is <b>when and how much</b>, not whether.</div><span class="dtexp-tag" style="color:#8a6d2e;background:#f6efe0">biggest lever · room to push</span></div>
    <div class="dtexp-card"><div class="dtexp-t"><span class="dtexp-num">2</span>Give the team value to sell</div><div class="dtexp-d">Upsell tied to usage the customer keeps — <b>users, warehouses, API, storage, AI</b> — and a second product that <b>sticks</b>: today customers buy Payroll/POS then drop it and keep Accounting, costing ~1pt of expansion. Without value levers from pricing &amp; product, a CS quota alone is empty.</div><span class="dtexp-tag">2nd product = ~1pt at stake</span></div>
    <div class="dtexp-card"><div class="dtexp-t"><span class="dtexp-num">3</span>Build a real expansion process</div><div class="dtexp-d">Today it's <b>reactive</b> — we sell when the customer asks. Build systematic account identification, <b>triggers</b>, qualification, offers and follow-up — the KAM / Account Management + CS motion. That turns upsell/cross-sell into something we generate, not something we wait for.</div><span class="dtexp-tag" style="color:#2f9e78;background:#e7f6f0">proactive · KAM + CS</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dtexp-take"><b>None of these levers work alone — expansion becomes structural only when pricing, product and process move together.</b> That's the shift: from ad hoc pushes to a system the commercial team can run every quarter.</div>
 </div>
 <div class="dtexp-foot"><span>Alegra · Confidential · pricing strategy — expansion</span><span>Expansion · 3/3</span></div>
</div>

